since 2004 Millennials have been voting  increasingly democratic in presidential  elections they're half as likely to  self-identify as politically  conservative and nearly twice as likely  to say they're liberal and this has led  many people to conclude that this is a  democratic generation but despite all of  this Millennials are actually no more  likely than Americans over 32 say  they're Democrats instead they're three  times as likely to say they're  independent and half as likely to say  they're Republican Millennials have  little confidence in either of the two  major political parties especially when  it comes to privacy government spending  the deficit and drug policy however they  do seem to view Democrats as the better  of two bad options and they're really  frustrated with the Republican Party who  they see as being a little bit too far  to the right on social issues the fact  is Millennials aren't liberals they're  social liberals and their fiscal  centrists and their social attitudes are  with largely defining their political  identities and driving their voting  behavior so how do we know that social  issues are largely defining their  political identities well first we ask  them among liberal Millennials only a  third said something about economics  being part of the reason why their  liberal instead two-thirds said  something about social issues social  tolerance is being critical to why they  are liberal second we find that  Millennials see themselves as closer to  President Barack Obama on social issues  no surprise there but they actually see  themselves as closer to New Jersey  Governor Chris Christie who is a  Republican on economic issues  nevertheless they're still voting for  Democrats the third thing is that a  majority of Millennials say that they  would vote for a socially liberal  fiscally conservative candidate some  might call that a libertarian leaning  candidate but the Millennials who are  most supportive of this type of  candidate are liberal and democratic  Millennials in fact liberal Millennials  are 20 points more likely than  conservatives to say that they would  vote for a socially liberal fiscally  conservative candidate these demonstrate  to us that social issues are largely  driving their political judgments  this actually isn't that surprising  because economics just isn't that  salient for this cohort yet and one of  some of the reasons for that are that  they're getting married later and  they're buying homes later and they're  just not paying that much in taxes yet  but once those things inevitably start  to happen economics will matter more to  their political judgments and we may see  a shift in their attitudes towards  government
 

